        "id": 85652,
        "name": "Braithwhite",
        "username": "Braithwhite",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/braithwhite/{size}/1854_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-11T18:20:47.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Why save the victims?</p><p>because he does damage to everyone if he kills them. And if he has his +1 damage drug out, he will deal 4 damage (in a 4 person game) at the end of the turn.&nbsp;</p><p>Many environments have +1 damage effects, and spite gets extra damage if you still have people in the safe house. So it is very easy, particularly as the number of players climbs, for him to do enough damage to trigger \"Collateral Damage\". &nbsp;If he has enough of a damage boost, it is quite easy for him to do 4 damage, trigger collateral damage, do 4 damage, trigger collateral damage, etc, etc, etc until all victims have been cleared.&nbsp;</p><p>I generally find large single hits easier to deal with than multiple AOE hits. I'll admit that this is a personal/play style preference!</p><p>a lot is going to depend on the environment, modifiers, and team dynamics. If you've got ways to tank and reduce his damage, then keeping hostages out is less of a danger. &nbsp;If you have people who are setup intensive, and Spite is undoing everything, then it makes more sense to keep stashing people to get more precious cards. &nbsp;If you have no ways of killing ongoings, and Collateral Damage comes out, then you might not want to tempt fate!</p><p>I'm not saying that there isn't a good reason to just keep all the victims in play, but generally I prefer not to so that I (and the people in my group) can get ahead of his drug card-back-to-hand curve, and punch through his armor!</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
